text:
Your list sorts fine, then another part of the app shows the wrong order. Often the cause: sort() changes the array in place.

Before:
const top = scores.sort((a, b) => b - a);
// scores is now reordered too

After:
const top = scores.toSorted((a, b) => b - a);
// scores is untouched

Per MDN, toSorted() is the copying version of sort(): it returns a new array and leaves the original alone. It has been available across browsers since July 2023.

It matters most with React state or props, where mutating the array in place can skip a re-render or reorder data other code still reads.

The same family exists for the other mutating methods: toReversed(), toSpliced() and with().

My rule of thumb: on shared data, reach for the copying method by default.

Have you ever chased a bug that turned out to be an in-place sort?
